{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And his sound shall be heard when he goes into the sanctuary.</b> The sound of Aaron — for when he goes into the sanctuary wearing the eight garments in this arrangement, his sound shall be heard and his prayer accepted. It further includes 'his sound' — the sound of the robe with the rattling of the bells. And this was a matter of conduct: the Torah taught proper conduct to a person who wishes to enter before the king, that he should knock at the doorway of the hall first, so as not to enter suddenly. The earthly kingdom is a likeness of the kingdom of the firmament: anyone who enters the king's hall suddenly is liable to death by the procedures of the kingdom, in the manner of what is written regarding King Ahasuerus (Esther 4:11): ״אשר לא יקרא אחת דתו להמית״. And there is another reason: so that it should be known that the high priest wishes to enter. And even though all is revealed and known before the Shekhinah, also before the angels who minister in the sanctuary before Him — and nothing is hidden from them either — the reason for the matter was so that the holy angels who stand before Him would not encounter him if he were to enter suddenly. And the matter of the bells was not to introduce new knowledge to the Shekhinah or to the angels there, but the matter necessarily encompasses great benefits: it is a warning to the angels to clear a place for the beloved of the King, for the King's honor, so that he can enter and serve Him in unity; and further, so that the priest would not be harmed upon entering there suddenly, and with this sign they would be driven away from before him and they would clear a place for the King's servant. And on his going out as well, the sign of calling was as though the priest were calling them to return to minister before Him as at first, for his service was already completed and he was going out. And it says ולא ימות, to teach that if he were to come there without making his sound heard and were to enter suddenly, he would die, for the ministers who are there around the Shekhinah would encounter him. And it is explained in the Derash of the Yerushalmi in tractate Yoma: ״וכל אדם לא יהיה באהל מועד״ — even those about whom it is written (Ezekiel 1:10) ״ודמות פניהם פני אדם״ — they shall not be in the Tent of Meeting; and therefore the priest was commanded to make a sound with the bells, in the manner of what is said (Psalms 55:15): ״אשר יחדו נמתיק סוד בבית אלהים נהלך ברגש״. And this sign of calling and requesting permission was all the days of the year, and this is what is said: ונשמע קולו בבאו אל הקדש — but not in the innermost sanctuary (לפני ולפנים), because in the innermost sanctuary he did not need to make a sound heard, nor did he enter there in golden garments but in white garments alone. And this is the merit of Israel: that the high priest would enter the innermost sanctuary on the Day of Atonement without the sign of calling and without requesting permission." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> What is the reason for the twelve tribes on the stones of the breastplate? That the Holy One, blessed be He, would look upon the garments of the priest upon his entry on the Day of Atonement and remember the merit of the tribes. R. Yehoshua ben Levi said: a parable — to a king's son who had a tutor; he would come before the king and advocate on behalf of the son, and he was afraid of those standing over him, lest they encounter him. What did he do? He dressed him in his own royal cloak, so that they would see him and be afraid of him. So too Aaron would enter at all times into the Holy of Holies, and had he not had many merits that would enter with him and assist him, he would not have been able to enter — for the ministering angels were there. What did the Holy One, blessed be He, do? He gave him a likeness of the garments of holiness, as is said (Isaiah 59:17): ״וילבש צדקה כשרין״. End."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, 